// `#[pattern(regex:)]`, Jakarta's `@Pattern` -- the one constraint docs/design/validation.md
// designed and deferred ("the ninth, and it arrives when something needs it"). It arrives as the
// first attribute added **after** `FIELD_ATTRS` was deleted (F250), so it is a line in
// `derive_validate.zm`'s own `attributes()` and a case in its `rule_for`, and **nothing in the
// compiler changed** -- which is what the extensibility was for.
//
// **Named `regex:`, not positional.** `#[pattern("^x$")]` would read fine with one argument and
// stop reading the moment a second existed, and `message:` is that second argument already --
// the reasoning `#[column(name:)]` was decided on.
//
// **The pattern is quoted on the way out**, like a `message:`. A regex is mostly backslashes:
// `"^\\d{3}$"` here is `^\d{3}$` by the time the lexer has finished, is re-escaped into the
// generated literal, and reaches `regex.compile` as `^\d{3}$` again. Getting that wrong would be
// invisible until a pattern with a `\d` in it stopped matching.
#[derive(Validate)]
struct Account {
    #[pattern(regex: "^[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{6}$")]
    sort_code: string

    // Both, and the message overrides only the one it is written on.
    #[not_blank]
    #[pattern(regex: "^\\d{3}-\\d{4}$", message: "must look like 123-4567")]
    phone: string

    // `Option`, so absence passes -- the rule's own overload, not something the attribute says.
    #[pattern(regex: "^[a-z]+$")]
    nickname: Option<string>
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
